$\newcommand{\bbS}{\mathbb{S}}
\newcommand{\mS}{\mathbf{S}}
\newcommand{\mD}{\mathbf{D}}
\newcommand{\mT}{\mathbf{T}}
\newcommand{\mW}{\mathbf{W}}
\newcommand{\mL}{\mathbf{L}}
\newcommand{\Exp}[1]{{\mathbb E}\left[#1\right]}$Dear Reviewer WhEe,

Thank you very much for your careful reviews and thoughtful questions for the paper. Below is a point-to-point response to your questions and concerns. 	

>*`As noted in the paper, one of the first matrix stepsize algorithms is the Newton's method. It is also highlighted the reason why this paper seeks a better matrix stepsize --- as computing and inverting the Hessian is always expensive. However, only the $\mT^k\mD\nabla f(x^k)$ case is easy to compute, and the $\mD\mS^k\nabla f(x^k)$ requires solving a convex program. It is important to quantify the computation complexity of the $DS^k\nabla f(x^k)$ case, otherwise it might just be better to use the Newton's method.`* & 
 *`Can you provide some implementation details for computing $D$ in the $DS^k\nabla f(x^k)$? What will be a practical algorithm and what is its runtime complexity?`*

We agree with the reviewer that det-CGD1 requires a convex matrix optimization problem to be solved and that in the most general case, it may be hard. However, the goal of this convex problem is to find the optimal matrix stepsize $\mD$, while our analysis is true for every $\mD$ that satisfies the condition (7). Finding a suboptimal stepsize $\mD$ is significantly easier. The procedure is described in Theorem 2 ($\ell=1$ case). In particular, for every $\mW\in\bbS^d_{++}$ we can find a scalar $\gamma$ such that $\mD=\gamma\mW$ satisfies the condition. Here, we want to emphasize that we do this computation at the start of the algorithm and therefore **we do it only once**. The complexity of finding such a scalar is equivalent to finding the largest eigenvalue of a matrix. In other words, if we choose a suboptimal $\mD$, then we outperform Newton’s method.  
Finally, our analysis and the experiments testify that a suboptimal $\mD$ chosen this way outperforms the standard CGD in terms of iteration and communication complexity. You can find these results in Table 1 and  Figures 2,3. 


>*`The sketching matrices $S^k$ and $T^k$ seem to be only useful when the problem has layerwise or blockwise structure. For a more general purpose optimization framework, how does one design such distributions? Perhaps this correlates to a recent work for first-order federated learning paradigm, where one first sketches the gradient, sends it to the server and uses the transpose of the sketches to recover the averaged gradients for model update [SWYZ22]?`*
&
>*`Authors should also discuss how to realize the operators $S^k$ and $T^k$ to realize communication cost reduction for general optimization tasks.`*

Indeed, designing the (distribution of) sketch matrix is important in our proposed method, as it directly determines the type of compression used in our algorithm. However, we want to first emphasize that the **sketching matrices here are not limited to block-wise structures**. In fact, the general case can be viewed as a special case where there is only $1$ block. As illustrated in the paper, a well designed sketch can bring compression for free (the case of Bernoulli sketches). Furthermore, there is a variety of sketches analyzed in [SHR21] that are relevant to our setting. 
The paper by SWYZ22 suggests a different sketch-and-de-sketch federated learning paradigm. This setting is not covered by our analysis, as the server in our case simply aggregates the compressed gradients and is somewhat orthogonal to the main focus of our paper. However, a combination of both methods remains an open direction for future work. *This discussion is added to the revised version of the paper.*

The recent paper [SWYZ22] also offers valuable insights in designing sketch matrices, specifically, it introduced an iterative sketching-based federated learning algorithm, which involved a novel sketch-and-de-sketch paradigm, allowing for the recovery of gradients after transmission from the client to the server. Convergence guarantees are provided given that the family of sketches satisfies the coordinate-wise embedding property. It is interesting to see how those sketches work in our matrix stepsize algorithm setting. A discussion of sketches and possible ways of designing them such as [SWYZ22] is added to the latest version of the paper.

>*`As the paper focuses on factoring the sketching matrices into analysis, more literature regarding such approach should be examined. For example, using sketching for second-order method in the case of linear program and empirical risk minimization has been heavily studied before [LSZ19, SY21, QSZZ23]. Can we devise matrix stepsize method for these interior point methods?`*


Sketch matrices are widely used as a compression technique in the setting of federated learning. Beyond that, sketch matrices also play important roles in other aspects of optimization such as regression and low-rank approximation, as the reviewer has pointed out. In addition, it can be used to speed up robust iterative methods such as the robust interior point method in [LSZ19] for solving empirical risk minimization problems. [SY21] introduced a new coordinate-wise embedding property to design sketch matrices, which can then be used in a slightly different way than [LSZ19] in combination with the central path method for solving linear programming problems. [QSZZ23] improved [LSZ19] by replacing the "sketching on the left" scheme with “sketching on the right”, which shares similar intuitions as our two versions of det-CGD algorithms here. This naturally leads to the possibility of devising matrix stepsize for interior point methods. However, this is orthogonal to the problem we focus on and a separate study should be conducted for a more in-depth understanding. A more detailed version of this discussion is added to the revision of the paper.

